# Redirect-only routes.
#
# Both also exist as vue-router entries, because an in-app click is handled
# client-side and never reaches the server. A crawler, a pasted link or a
# search result does reach it, and must get a real 301 rather than the SPA
# shell — which is why these cannot live in the router alone.
#
# They used to be disallowed in robots.txt instead. That is the one thing that
# does NOT work: Google found /apidoc through the sitewide footer link, was
# forbidden to fetch it, and so could never learn it was only a signpost — it
# indexed the bare URL and reported "Indexed, though blocked by robots.txt"
# (Search Console, 18.08.2026). A URL has to be crawlable for its 301 to be
# seen; blocking a page is not a way to keep it out of the index.
/apidoc  /api/api-docs/  301
/github  https://github.com/jensbuehl/aoe4-guides  301

# The API proxy, deliberately narrow.
#
# This site needs exactly one API route: a single build by id, which
# buildService.getBuildFromApi calls when the browser could not produce an App
# Check token and Firestore refused the read. It never calls the list endpoint.
#
# /api/* used to forward everything, which lent the whole service surface a
# first-party address - list endpoints, favorites, every filter combination,
# reachable under the site's own domain and looking endorsed by it. Narrowing
# does not block anyone (the service answers on its own hostname either way);
# it makes this path exactly as wide as the site needs, which is what lets the
# two surfaces be treated differently later - the site path anonymous and dull,
# the documented API keyed and identifiable.
#
# Rules are matched top to bottom, so the closing catch-all must stay last, and
# the whole block must stay above /* or the SPA shell swallows it.
/api/apidocs      https://aoe4-guides-api-7h2vti5ckq-ey.a.run.app/api-docs/         200
/api/api-docs     https://aoe4-guides-api-7h2vti5ckq-ey.a.run.app/api-docs/         200
/api/api-docs/*   https://aoe4-guides-api-7h2vti5ckq-ey.a.run.app/api-docs/:splat   200
/api/builds/:id   https://aoe4-guides-api-7h2vti5ckq-ey.a.run.app/builds/:id        200

# Anything else under /api answers with a JSON 404 naming the direct address,
# rather than the SPA shell with status 200. A caller that breaks here has to be
# told why and where to go instead: the published OpenAPI spec still advertises
# https://aoe4guides.com/api as its base, so a documented list call lands here.
/api/*            /api-not-proxied.json                                             404

/* /index.html 200
